  "id": "Compound::DB00540 - Gene::1132",
  "verbalization": "Nortriptyline, a tricyclic antidepressant, exhibits a strong binding interaction with Chrm2 and Chrm3, both of which are part of the G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor family. These receptors are involved in the regulation of neurotransmitter signaling, and their participation in the G-protein coupled neurotransmitter receptor activity pathway is linked to Chrm4. The evidence suggests that Nortriptyline may modulate Chrm4 through a mechanism involving the G-protein coupled receptor pathway, potentially influencing signaling pathways related to acetylcholine receptor function. The consistent binding to Chrm2 and Chrm3, along with their shared participation in the same receptor activity, supports a mechanism where Nortriptyline affects Chrm4 via a common downstream pathway. The integrated evidence implies that Nortriptyline may exert its effects on Chrm4 through a G-protein coupled receptor-mediated mechanism, though further experimental validation is required to confirm the direct interaction and specificity of this effect.",
